    "body": "Hey — I built a stateful Telegram Bot API emulator as part of [vercel-labs/emulate](https://github.com/vercel-labs/emulate). It's an HTTP server that speaks the real Bot API, so a grammY bot runs against it unmodified:\n\n```ts\nnew Bot(token, { client: { apiRoot: \"http://localhost:4007\" } });\n```\n\nNo real bot token, no network, no second Telegram account. Tests provision bots + chats, drive scripted user activity (messages, photos, callbacks, reactions, edits, membership changes), and assert on bot replies.\n\nThe PR already includes a grammY demo under `examples/telegram-grammy/` — a production-shaped grammY bot with 9 parity tests that boot the emulator in-process and round-trip real scenarios (~60s).\n\nCovers `getUpdates` + webhooks (with `secret_token`, retry on 5xx, concurrent-poll 409), all the common `send*` methods, `editMessageText` / `editMessageReplyMarkup` / `deleteMessage`, `setMessageReaction`, `answerCallbackQuery`, inline keyboards, `getFile` + file download, MarkdownV2/HTML with UTF-16 entity offsets, forum topics, channels, supergroups, Privacy Mode in groups, fault injection for 401/403/404/429 retry paths. Real Telegram error wording on 4096/1024 length, unescaped MarkdownV2, missing reply target, etc. Non-goals (payments, games, Business API, inline mode, polls, media groups) listed in the README.\n\nFramework-agnostic — telegraf users can point at it too. If anyone wants tighter grammY ergonomics, a thin wrapper (Vitest hooks, typed `Context` helpers) would be ~200 lines of glue on top.\n\nCurious what bot flows you'd want tested that aren't covered.\n\n- PR: https://github.com/vercel-labs/emulate/pull/75\n- README: https://github.com/vercel-labs/emulate/blob/feat/telegram-emulator/packages/%40emulators/telegram/README.md\n- grammY demo: https://github.com/vercel-labs/emulate/tree/feat/telegram-emulator/examples/telegram-grammy",
